 "title": "Amazing grace! (how sweet the sound) · Hymns · Bible Guide",
 "heading": "Amazing grace how sweet the sound",
 "paragraph": "John Newton printed it in 1779 in Olney Hymns, the hymn book he made with William Cowper, under a verse from 1 Chronicles where David asks how God has brought him this far.",
 "blocks": [
  {
   "tag": "p",
   "text": "STUDY / LIVING THE CHRISTIAN LIFE / HYMNS"
  },
  {
   "tag": "h1",
   "text": "Amazing grace how sweet the sound"
  },
  {
   "tag": "p",
   "text": "John Newton printed it in 1779 in Olney Hymns, the hymn book he made with William Cowper, under a verse from 1 Chronicles where David asks how God has brought him this far."
  },
  {
   "tag": "p",
   "text": "WRITTEN BY"
  },
  {
   "tag": "p",
   "text": "John Newton 1725–1807"
  },
  {
   "tag": "p",
   "text": "PRINTED"
  },
  {
   "tag": "p",
   "text": "1779 Olney Hymns · Book I, no. 41"
  },
  {
   "tag": "p",
   "text": "METRE"
  },
  {
   "tag": "p",
   "text": "Common Metre 8.6.8.6 · 6 stanzas"
  },
  {
   "tag": "p",
   "text": "IN HYMNALS"
  },
  {
   "tag": "p",
   "text": "1,811 listed at Hymnary.org"
  },
  {
   "tag": "h2",
   "text": "The hymn, and the Scripture in it"
  },
  {
   "tag": "p",
   "text": "All six stanzas as Newton printed them. Beside the lines that echo a verse, the verse hymnal editors attach to the hymn. Hover a line or a verse to join them; click to read the passage. Below the hymn, the same links as a constellation."
  },
  {
   "tag": "p",
   "text": "11 echoes in 24 lines."
  },
  {
   "tag": "p",
   "text": "AMONG THE SCRIPTURES"
  },
  {
   "tag": "p",
   "text": "The six stanzas at the centre; around them, every book hymnal editors tie the hymn to, Old Testament on the left and New on the right, sized by how many of its passages they name. Choose a stanza or a book."
  },
  {
   "tag": "p",
   "text": "a line echoes this verse hymnal editors tie the hymn here"
  },
  {
   "tag": "p",
   "text": "STANZA I"
  },
  {
   "tag": "h3",
   "text": "Amazing grace! (how sweet the sound)"
  },
  {
   "tag": "p",
   "text": "That saved a wretch like me!"
  },
  {
   "tag": "p",
   "text": "I once was lost, but now am found,"
  },
  {
   "tag": "p",
   "text": "Was blind, but now I see."
  },
  {
   "tag": "h2",
   "text": "Where Newton found it"
  },
  {
   "tag": "p",
   "text": "He printed the hymn under David's prayer after God's promise of a lasting house. David asks why God has brought him \"this far\"; the hymn answers that grace has brought me \"safe thus far\"."
  },
  {
   "tag": "p",
   "text": "Then King David went in, sat before the LORD, and said, “Who am I, O LORD God, and what is my house, that You have brought me this far? 1 CHRONICLES 17:16 · BEREAN STANDARD BIBLE"
  },
  {
   "tag": "p",
   "text": "Through many dangers, toils and snares, I have already come; ’Tis grace has brought me safe thus far, and grace will lead me home. STANZA III"
  },
  {
   "tag": "h2",
   "text": "Its place in the book"
  },
  {
   "tag": "p",
   "text": "Book I of Olney Hymns walks through the Bible from Genesis to Revelation, one hymn per passage: 141 hymns. This is number 41, the only one on 1 Chronicles. (Across all three books Newton wrote 281 hymns, Cowper 68.) Hover a tick to see the hymn."
  },
  {
   "tag": "p",
   "text": "NO. 41 · 1 CHRONICLES · JOHN NEWTON Amazing grace! (how sweet the sound)"
  },
  {
   "tag": "h2",
   "text": "Where hymnals place it"
  },
  {
   "tag": "p",
   "text": "Printed hymnals list, for each hymn, the Bible passages it goes with, so a church can choose a hymn for the day's reading. Gathered from the hymnals Hymnary.org catalogues, editors have tied this hymn to 99 passages in 30 books. One square is one passage; a ringed square is one the hymn's own lines echo. Point at a square to read it; click to open it beside the page."
  },
